{
  "predictions": [
    {
      "claim_id": "artifact_access_helps",
      "probability": 0.88,
      "rationale": "Artifact probing provides an additional and highly discriminative signal. On the public test set, artifact-only achieves AUC=1.0 and TPR=1.0 at FPR=0.0, while trajectory-only achieves AUC=0.993 and TPR=0.833. The combined score (AUC=1.0) is at least as good as either alone. The directional claim that artifact access improves monitoring under controlled FPR is robustly supported on public data. Uncertainty comes from possible hidden attack families with small trigger-drops that evade the artifact probe."
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "post_training_is_hardest",
      "probability": 0.45,
      "rationale": "On the public calibration data, post_training cases (delayed_disable, selective_filter families) are not consistently harder to detect than other families. In fact, calibration has 4 delayed_disable and 4 selective_filter cases, all detected. The hardest cases are low-intensity selective_filter cases (pub-test-004, pub-test-023) which appear in both post_training and optimization settings. Insufficient evidence to confidently rank post_training as hardest."
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "persistence_beats_max_only",
      "probability": 0.72,
      "rationale": "On the public test set, trajectory-only with persistence (AUC=0.993) outperforms max-only standalone trajectory (AUC=0.958). However, when combined with artifact, both combined and combined_no_persistence achieve AUC=1.0 with the same TPR/FPR. So persistence helps for trajectory-only but provides marginal benefit when artifact probing is available. The directional claim holds for the trajectory-only ablation, which is the primary comparison."
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "artifact_only_is_sufficient",
      "probability": 0.70,
      "rationale": "Artifact-only monitoring achieves perfect performance (AUC=1.0, TPR=1.0, FPR=0.0) on the public test set, strictly better than trajectory-only. However, this is based on a small public test set and may not generalize to hidden attack families where artifacts have smaller trigger drops. The combined monitor is more robust in principle. Artifact-only sufficiency is likely on seen families but uncertain for novel attacks."
    }
  ]
}

